@startuml
title Oracle failure modes and what bounds each one
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam PackageBackgroundColor #F7FAFF
skinparam PackageBorderColor #4A78B5
skinparam ComponentBackgroundColor #EEF5FF
skinparam ComponentBorderColor #4A78B5
skinparam ComponentFontColor #1F3A5F
skinparam ArrowColor #4A78B5
skinparam ArrowFontColor #1F3A5F
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #F7FAFF
skinparam NoteBorderColor #4A78B5

package "Attack or failure" {
  [Manipulate one spot venue] as F1
  [Manipulate Hyperliquid's own book] as F2
  [A minority of validators\npublish a false price] as F3
  [A stake majority colludes] as F4
  [An HIP-3 deployer publishes\na wrong or manipulated price] as F5
  [No external price exists\nfor the asset] as F6
}

package "What bounds it" {
  [Weighted median over 8 venues;\none venue is at most weight 3 of 12] as B1
  [Mark price is a median over three\nindices, only one of which is the book] as B2
  [Stake-weighted median across\nthe validator set] as B3
  [Nothing in the oracle. This is the\nbase HyperBFT assumption] as B4
  [Validator review on any 50% move in\nexternalPerpPx; 500k HYPE slashable\nand burned] as B5
  [Hyperps: oracle is an 8 h EWMA of own\nmarks, capped at 4x the initial mark] as B6
}

F1 --> B1
F2 --> B2
F3 --> B3
F4 --> B4
F5 --> B5
F6 --> B6

note bottom of B4
  Every other row is bounded by construction.
  This one is not, and it is the reason the
  security of the oracle is the security of
  the stake distribution.
end note
@enduml
